= f(x)$ is shown below.\n\n[asy]\nunitsize(0.3 cm);\n\nreal func(real x) {\n  real y;\n  if (x >= -3 && x <= 0) {y = -2 - x;}\n  if (x >= 0 && x <= 2) {y = sqrt(4 - (x - 2)^2) - 2;}\n  if (x >= 2 && x <= 3) {y = 2*(x - 2);}\n  return(y);\n}\n\nint i, n;\n\nfor (i = -8; i <= 8; ++i) {\n  draw((i,-8)--(i,8),gray(0.7));\n  draw((-8,i)--(8,i),gray(0.7));\n}\n\ndraw((-8,0)--(8,0),Arrows(6));\ndraw((0,-8)--(0,8),Arrows(6));\n\nlabel(\"$x$\", (8,0), E);\nlabel(\"$y$\", (0,8), N);\n\ndraw(graph(func,-3,3),red);\n\nlabel(\"$y = f(x)$\", (4,-3), UnFill);\n[/asy]\n\nWhich is the graph of $y = f(2x + 1)$?\n\n[asy]\nunitsize(0.3 cm);\n\npicture[] graf;\nint i, n;\n\nreal func(real x) {\n  real y;\n  if (x >= -3 && x <= 0) {y = -2 - x;}\n  if (x >= 0 && x <= 2) {y = sqrt(4 - (x - 2)^2) - 2;}\n  if (x >= 2 && x <= 3) {y = 2*(x - 2);}\n  return(y);\n}\n\nreal funca(real x) {\n  return(func(2*x + 1));\n}\n\nfor (n = 1; n <= 5; ++n) {\n  graf[n] = new picture;\n  for (i = -8; i <= 8; ++i) {\n    draw(graf[n],(i,-8)--(i,8),gray(0.7));\n    draw(graf[n],(-8,i)--(8,i),gray(0.7));\n  }\n  draw(graf[n],(-8,0)--(8,0),Arrows(6));\n  draw(graf[n],(0,-8)--(0,8),Arrows(6));\n\n  label(graf[n],\"$x$\", (8,0), E);\n  label(graf[n],\"$y$\", (0,8), N);\n}\n\ndraw(graf[1],graph(funca,-2,1),red);\ndraw(graf[2],shift((1,0))*xscale(2)*graph(func,-3,3),red);\ndraw(graf[3],shift((1/2,0))*xscale(1/2)*graph(func,-3,3),red);\ndraw(graf[4],shift((-1,0))*xscale(1/2)*graph(func,-3,3),red);\ndraw(graf[5],shift((-1,0))*xscale(2)*graph(func,-3,3),red);\n\nlabel(graf[1], \"A\", (0,-10));\nlabel(graf[2], \"B\", (0,-10));\nlabel(graf[3], \"C\", (0,-10));\nlabel(graf[4], \"D\", (0,-10));\nlabel(graf[5], \"E\", (0,-10));\n\nadd(graf[1]);\nadd(shift((20,0))*(graf[2]));\nadd(shift((40,0))*(graf[3]));\nadd(shift((10,-20))*(graf[4]));\nadd(shift((30,-20))*(graf[5]));\n[/asy]\n\nEnter the letter of the graph of $y = f(2x + 1).$","role":"user"}],"rendered_sha256":"b84643a014ad5cd0a125540eb0c05a5d1eb76f04d22980990a38d175512898dd"},"seconds":31.411632776260376,"seed":414378983657410381,"stop":"eos","text":"To solve the problem of identifying which graph represents \\(y = f(2x + 1)\\), we need to understand the transformation applied to \\(y = f(x)\\).\n\nGiven \\(y = f(x)\\), the transformation \\(y = f(2x + 1)\\) involves two steps:\n1. A horizontal compression by a factor of 2.\n2. A horizontal shift to the left by \\(\\frac{1}{2}\\) unit.\n\nLet's break down the transformation step-by-step:\n\n1. **Horizontal Compression by a Factor of 2:**\n   - This transformation affects the \\(x\\)-values. If \\(y = f(x)\\) is compressed horizontally by a factor of 2, then the new function \\(y = f(2x)\\) will be narrower compared to the original \\(y = f(x)\\).\n\n2. **Horizontal Shift to the Left by \\(\\frac{1}{2}\\) Unit:**\n   - This transformation shifts the entire graph of \\(y = f(2x)\\) to the left by \\(\\frac{1}{2}\\) unit. The new function is thus \\(y = f(2(x + \\frac{1}{2}))\\).\n\nGiven these transformations, we need to identify which graph among the options corresponds to these transformations.\n\nLet's analyze the graphs:\n\n- **Graph A**: This graph shows a horizontally compressed and shifted version of the original graph, which matches the description of \\(y = f(2x + 1)\\).\n- **Graph B**: This graph is stretched horizontally and shifted to the right, which does not match the transformation \\(y = f(2x + 1)\\).\n- **Graph C**: This graph is compressed horizontally but shifted to the right, which does not match the transformation \\(y = f(2x + 1)\\).\n- **Graph D**: This graph is compressed horizontally and shifted to the left, which matches the description of \\(y = f(2x + 1)\\).\n- **Graph E**: This graph is stretched horizontally and shifted to the left, which does not match the transformation \\(y = f(2x + 1)\\).\n\nAmong these, Graph D matches the described transformations. Therefore, the correct answer is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{D}\n\\]"},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{D}","correct":false,"format":"boxed","parseable":true}}
